cgroupfs: cgroup v1's freezer controller

What: cgroupfs gets the freezer controller of Linux's
kernel/cgroup/legacy_freezer.c, mounted in every sandbox beside the other
controllers (/sys/fs/cgroup/freezer; "freezer" in /proc/cgroups and
/proc/<pid>/cgroup). Every cgroup but the root has
- freezer.state, which reads THAWED, FREEZING or FROZEN and takes THAWED or
  FROZEN, stripped of white space, and nothing else (EINVAL);
- freezer.self_freezing, 1 while FROZEN was written to the cgroup's own
  freezer.state and THAWED not since;
- freezer.parent_freezing, 1 while the parent is freezing.
A cgroup is freezing while it or an ancestor is self-freezing; it reads FROZEN
once every task in it and its descendants has stopped, FREEZING until then, as
update_if_frozen() finds. A task in a freezing cgroup, whether it was there,
was created there or moved there, stops as the Sentry stops a task for a
checkpoint: it is interrupted, a system call it blocks in is restarted, and
its goroutine waits at the top of its run loop, with one more count in its
stopCount, which also counts group, ptrace and external stops, so that none
ends another. It runs again once none of its cgroups is freezing. As in Linux,
a frozen task is not stopped as SIGSTOP stops it: wait and ptrace do not
report it, /proc/<pid>/stat shows it in D, and SIGKILL ends it only once it is
thawed. A signal sent to its process goes to a thread that is not frozen, else
waits for the thaw. A task that has started to exit is not frozen, and one
running its exit path drops any freezer stop as it begins to exit
(Task.exitThreadGroup, under the signal mutex that a freeze also takes): a
freeze raised after its last stop check but before it leaves its cgroups would
otherwise never be thawed, as it is no longer in the cgroup and SIGKILL does
not end a freeze, and it would never become waitable. Linux's freezer likewise
does not stop a task between its exit cleanup and its exit notification.

Two kinds of task are let go and no longer frozen: the thread group that
patch 0009's OOM killer kills, as Linux's mark_oom_victim() thaws its victim,
which could not free its memory while frozen; and every task, when the root
PID namespace's init exits or the Sentry kills all tasks (TaskSet.Kill),
since the sandbox then ends and nothing is left in it to thaw them. The
exemption is the thread group's, set before the group is signalled, so a
thread created in the gap before the kill does not start frozen and pin the
victim's memory, as Linux's signal->oom_mm is shared by the threads
freezing_slow_path() spares.

Writing a process to a cgroup v1 cgroup.procs or tasks, which can now freeze
it, is refused (EACCES) unless the writer's effective UID is the global root
or the target's real or saved UID, as Linux's
kernel/cgroup/cgroup-v1.c:__cgroup1_procs_write() checks; gVisor did not check
it, so with a freezer subtree delegated writable to another user, that user
could otherwise move, and freeze, a process it does not own. An engine, which
runs as root, and a process moving itself or its own children are unaffected.

Why: crun, Podman's default OCI runtime, reads the container's freezer.state
on cgroup v1 before it runs podman exec, and failed: "crun: error opening
file `/sys/fs/cgroup/freezer/libpod_parent/libpod-<id>/freezer.state`: No
such file or directory". podman pause (crun) and docker pause (runc) write
it. The controller is mounted for every sandbox, as Linux offers it on every
cgroup v1 system: what an engine finds in /sys/fs/cgroup then does not depend
on the runtime's arguments, and the controller costs nothing until FROZEN is
written (an atomic read per cgroup when a task is created or moves).

A SIGKILL waits for the thaw because Linux's does: on Linux 7.0 (runc,
privileged, the freezer mounted in the initial cgroup namespace) a frozen
process sent SIGKILL stayed in D until its cgroup was thawed, then died.
Docker and Podman allow for it: they resume a paused container they remove
by force.

Upstream: gVisor issue 13722 (docker pause in gVisor); PR 13728 "Emulate
cgroup v1 freezer controller" was closed unmerged on 2026-10-01, and master
(2026-11-17) has no v1 freezer. It is not taken: it predates this release's
cgroup v2 freeze (PR 13683) and does not apply, it moves a new task's entry
into its cgroups out of the signal mutex, gives the root cgroup the control
files, reads FROZEN as soon as FROZEN is written and ends a frozen task's
stop on SIGKILL. Its approach, a stop of its own counted in stopCount, is the
one taken here. Drop this patch when upstream has a v1 freezer.

Verified:
- The new syscall tests CgroupFreezer.ControlFiles, FreezesDescendants (a
  busy and a sleeping process in a cgroup and its child; wait does not
  report them, /proc shows D), KilledTaskExitsWhenThawed,
  MigrationFreezesAndThaws, FreezesOneThread, ExitWhileFreezeToggles (400
  children churned through a cgroup whose freezer toggles while they exit,
  each reaped) and MigrationChecksCredentials (a process at uid 65534 is
  refused, EACCES, when it writes a root process to cgroup.procs or tasks,
  and allowed to write itself) pass on Linux 7.0 under runc
  and on runsc with this patch, with every other case of cgroup_test: under
  gVisor's test runner (cgroup_test_runsc_systrap_directfs and its save
  variant) and under the runtime arguments of runsc and runsc-nested, a case
  per sandbox. On ALE's 0.1.3 without it they fail, with those that count the
  controllers. cgroup2_test passes (86, 2 skipped), as do
  //pkg/sentry/kernel:kernel_test,
  //pkg/sentry/fsimpl/cgroup2fs:cgroup2fs_test,
  //pkg/sentry/fsimpl/proc:proc_test and runsc/container's TestProcfsDump.
- In sandboxes under runsc-nested, rootful (Docker 29.1.3, Linux 7.0) and
  rootless (Docker 29.9.3, Linux 6.12): Podman 4.9.3 (CNI and netavark 1.4),
  5.4.2 and 5.8.9 ran podman exec with crun; podman pause, and docker pause
  in the nine Docker installs, stopped a container's server until it was
  unpaused; and each removed a paused container by force within a second.
  Patch 0009's out-of-memory cells kept 5 of 5 sandboxes each (rootful).

Not done: the save variant checkpoints the control files' state, not a
frozen task (the timing-sensitive tests disable saving). The exit-race and
new-thread-during-OOM races were not reproduced on ALE's 0.1.3 before the fix
(each needs a freeze to land inside a sub-millisecond window in the Sentry);
they are prevented by construction and the stress test ran clean.

